course: dictionary
lesson: 8119
title: Repay: paying back
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To repay means to pay back money you borrowed. «To borrow is to take money you must give back; debt is the money you still owe; to repay is to pay it back.» Look for the word back: you give the money back.
explain: Repay can also mean doing something good for someone who helped you. If a friend shares her lunch with you, you might repay her with a kind act. Ask yourself: what did this person do for me, and what could I do in return?
explain: Repay can also be about effort. If you practise hard for weeks and then win, you could say your hard work repaid itself, meaning it was worth all the effort. Read the sentence around the word to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Repay means to pay back money you borrowed, or to give something back in return for help.
fact: Debt is the money you still owe.
fact: «Missed sleep adds up as sleep debt; a weekend lie-in helps a little but can't repay a whole week.» A long lie-in does not fully pay back lost sleep.
fact: Banks lend money, but first they check the borrower can pay it back, because «a loan that is never repaid is lost money».
fact: The forms of repay are repays, repaid and repaying. Repaid is the past tense: «I repaid the loan last week.»
exercise: «To borrow is to take money you must give back.» What does repay mean? | To spend money on sweets | To lend money to a friend | *To pay back money you borrowed | To count money carefully
exercise: «A loan that is never repaid is lost money.» What does repaid mean here? | *Paid back | Given away | Written down | Spent on sweets
exercise: «Missed sleep adds up as sleep debt; a weekend lie-in helps a little but can't repay a whole week.» Does a lie-in fully repay lost sleep? | Yes, it always repays every hour | No, because sleep is not money | Yes, if you sleep on Sunday | *No, it helps only a little
test: Jay borrowed 5 pounds from his sister on Monday. What must he do? | Lend it to a stranger | Hide it under his bed | Eat the money | *Repay it by paying the money back
test: Mia helped Sam with his maths. Sam wants to repay her. What could he do? | Forget about it forever | *Do something kind for her in return | Borrow more money from her | Tell her the maths was easy
exam: Which meaning of repay fits best in «Banks check that people can repay their loans»? | Build a new house | Give a prize to the winner | *Pay back what was borrowed | Carry heavy boxes
